        "learnerExperience": "Learners listen to a one-to-two-minute narrated audio clip describing the Lake Victoria fisherman scenario (thunder heard after the lightning flash, sound seemingly coming from behind a rocky cliff, water waves bending near the shore). They simultaneously examine a projected or printed photograph of the Kisumu shoreline during stormy weather. Each learner then writes, individually and in silence, their personal prediction in their exercise book, answering the prompt: WHY can the fisherman hear thunder from behind the cliff, and why do the water waves change direction near the shore? Learners are given a full three minutes of silent writing time before any sharing begins. After writing, six to eight learners are cold-called to share their predictions aloud. The teacher records each prediction verbatim on the DQB using a different colour for each prediction category (sound-related, water-related, energy-related, other).",
        "teacherMoves": "Before playing the audio, the teacher says: BEFORE I play this clip, I want you to look at this photograph of the shores of Lake Victoria near Kisumu. Notice the rocky headland, the shallow bay, and the open lake. Keep that image in your mind. The teacher plays the audio clip, then says: Now write in your book: explain in your own words why you think the fisherman can hear the thunder even though the cliff is in the way, and why the water waves seem to change direction. You have three minutes. Write alone. No talking yet. [WAIT TIME: 3 minutes of silent individual writing.] After writing, the teacher says: I am going to call on some of you. I want to hear YOUR thinking, not a neighbour's. [Cold-calls minimum six students, distributing across the room.] After each response the teacher probes with: What in your everyday experience makes you think that? or Can anyone add to what [name] has said without repeating it? The teacher does NOT confirm or reject any prediction at this stage, responding instead with: That is an interesting idea. Let us put it on our board and see what the science tells us by the end of this unit.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Elicit-then-compare predictions strategy: by recording all predictions publicly on the DQB without evaluation, the teacher surfaces prior conceptions (for example, sound bouncing versus sound bending versus sound travelling through the cliff) and creates productive cognitive conflict. Learners can see that the class holds multiple competing ideas, which motivates the need for scientific vocabulary and investigation.",
