        "teacherMoves": "As learners observe the unlabelled diagram the teacher says: Look carefully at these two wave patterns. In 60 seconds, tell your partner: what do you notice about the shape, and what is different about how the disturbance moves? [WAIT TIME: 60 seconds.] After pairs share, the teacher says: You have described what you see. Scientists have specific names for every feature you just pointed at. That is what you are going to discover now. During the slinky demonstration the teacher says: Watch this carefully. I am not explaining yet. Just observe. [Performs transverse pulse.] What did you see? [WAIT TIME: 30 seconds. Cold-calls two students.] [Performs longitudinal pulse.] And this one? How is it different? [WAIT TIME: 30 seconds. Cold-calls two different students.] The teacher does not provide the vocabulary during the Observe phase, preserving learner discovery for the Explain phase.",
        "sensemakingStrategy": "Notice-and-wonder observation protocol: by withholding labels during observation, the teacher trains learners to describe physical phenomena in their own language first. This builds observational science skills and ensures that when vocabulary is introduced in the Explain phase, it is anchored to a concrete visual and physical referent rather than an abstract definition.",
        "formativeAssessment": "Listen to pair-share conversations for evidence that learners can distinguish the direction of particle motion from the direction of wave travel. A learner who says the slinky moves side to side but the wave moves forward is demonstrating the conceptual core of transverse waves. Use this as a benchmark: if fewer than half the class articulates this distinction, slow down in the Explain phase and add a second slinky demonstration."
