        "teacherMoves": "The teacher distributes reference cards (or directs learners to the relevant textbook pages) and says: You now have eight terms to find and understand. Do not just copy a definition. Read it, look at your slinky sketch, and then write what it means in your own words first. Then add the formal definition. You have seven minutes. [Circulates, checking progress, asking probing questions such as: How does wavelength differ from amplitude? Could a very loud sound have a short wavelength? What decides the period of a wave?] After seven minutes the teacher says: Now build your diagram. Both wave types on the same page. Label every part. Then add your Kenyan examples as annotations. You have six minutes. [WAIT TIME between prompts: 30 seconds.] During the whole-class share-out the teacher cold-calls four students to each contribute one label to the master diagram, asking: [Name], come and label one feature and tell us what it means in the context of the fisherman on Lake Victoria. The teacher closes the Explain phase by saying: Every scientific investigation needs a shared language. From today, these eight terms are OUR language for understanding how the fisherman hears thunder from behind the cliff.",
