      "title": "Diffraction of Waves — Sound Around the Corner and Signals Over the Hill",
      "observed": "Students observed ripple-tank wavefronts diffracting around obstacles and through apertures, confirming that diffraction is most pronounced when the gap width or obstacle size is comparable to the wavelength of the wave.",
      "learned": "Students constructed wavefront diagrams showing that waves spread (diffract) around obstacles and through gaps, and that diffraction is greatest when the wavelength is approximately equal to (or larger than) the gap or obstacle size. They applied this principle to: (a) sound diffraction — sound waves have wavelengths of centimetres to metres, so they easily diffract around doorways, furniture, and corners in Kenyan classrooms and markets; (b) radio wave diffraction — long-wavelength KBC AM/FM signals diffract over hills, providing coverage in remote Kenyan valleys, whereas short-wavelength TV signals diffract poorly and are blocked by terrain, requiring relay stations; (c) ocean wave diffraction — waves diffracting around headlands (e.g., Ras Serani near Mombasa) can focus energy on sheltered beaches. Pedagogical note: require learners to draw at least two labelled wavefront diagrams (one showing little diffraction through a wide gap, one showing strong diffraction through a narrow gap) before moving to applications.",
      "explained": "Diffraction is explained by Huygens' principle: every point on a wavefront acts as a new point source of secondary wavelets. When a wavefront passes through a narrow gap, only a small portion of the wavefront remains, and the secondary wavelets at the edges radiate outward into the geometric shadow, producing spreading. When the gap is much wider than λ, the secondary wavelets from the edges are a tiny fraction of the total wavefront and their effect is negligible — the wave continues in a straight beam with only minor edge spreading. When gap ≈ λ, edge effects dominate and the wave fans out in all forward directions. This explains the Aberdare scenario: KBC longwave signals (λ ~ hundreds of metres) easily diffract over the hill; TV signals (λ ~ centimetres) travel in near-straight lines and are blocked. Teachers should connect this forward to Lesson 7: diffraction of ocean waves around coastal structures can unexpectedly concentrate wave energy on supposedly sheltered beaches — a critical environmental mitigation insight."
