      "observed": "Students observed a carousel of application demonstrations and/or short video clips: (1) a sonar/ultrasound animation showing a pulse sent from a vessel and the echo timed to calculate depth — linked to Kenya Fisheries vessels on Lake Victoria; (2) a medical ultrasound image (e.g., of a foetus) from Kenyatta National Hospital, showing how reflected sound pulses build an image; (3) a microwave oven cut-away diagram showing how microwaves reflect and refract inside the cavity to heat ugali or a chapati evenly; (4) a diagram of optical fibre communication used in Kenya's National Broadband network (the undersea cable at Mombasa) — total internal reflection carrying data as light pulses; (5) a seismograph trace from the Kenya Meteorological Department showing P-waves and S-waves from a tremor near the Rift Valley.",
      "learned": "Each real-life Kenyan application deliberately exploits one or more wave properties. SONAR and ultrasound use REFLECTION — a directed pulse is sent out, the reflected echo is timed, and distance = (v × t)/2 is applied. Medical ultrasound at KNH uses the same principle to image internal tissues. Microwave ovens exploit the specific frequency at which water molecules resonate (absorption) and reflection from metal walls to distribute energy. Fibre-optic cables (used in Kenya's TEAMS undersea cable terminating at Mombasa) exploit total internal reflection — light repeatedly reflects inside the glass fibre, carrying data over thousands of kilometres with minimal loss. Seismology uses the refraction and reflection of P- and S-waves through Earth's interior to map geological structures — critical for Kenya's Rift Valley geothermal energy exploration (KenGen). Pedagogical note: for each application, ask learners to identify WHICH wave property (reflection, refraction, diffraction, or a combination) is being exploited before revealing the explanation.",
      "explained": "The unifying explanation across all applications is that engineers and scientists do not fight wave behaviour — they engineer systems that harness it. Reflection is exploited wherever distance measurement or imaging is needed (sonar, ultrasound, radar). Refraction is exploited in lens design and in understanding how radio signals propagate through the atmosphere over the Kenyan highlands. Diffraction is exploited in antenna design — a dish antenna is sized so that the target wavelength diffracts minimally, keeping the beam tight (microwave links) or, conversely, antenna arrays are designed for maximum diffraction for broad-area FM coverage across rural Kenya. Total internal reflection (a consequence of refraction at critical angle) enables fibre-optic internet infrastructure. Students should be able to state: 'In [application], the [property] of waves is exploited because [mechanism].' Teachers should cold-call at least five students using this sentence frame before closing the lesson."
