      "learned": "Each environmental impact is caused by a specific, nameable wave property: diffraction concentrates wave energy on unprotected shorelines (waves diffracting around a harbour breakwater can converge on the sheltered side); high-amplitude waves (tsunamis, storm surges) carry enormous mechanical energy that erodes coasts (Malindi, Watamu); seismic waves (P- and S-waves from Rift Valley tremors) transfer energy through Earth's crust, damaging poorly constructed buildings. Mitigation strategies are matched to the causative property: breakwaters and mangrove restoration reduce wave amplitude before waves reach the shore; early-warning buoys (Indian Ocean Tsunami Warning System, to which Kenya subscribes) detect anomalous wave patterns and trigger alerts; building codes in Nairobi require seismic-resistant designs in earthquake-prone zones near the Rift; sound-absorbing materials reduce noise pollution in urban schools. Pedagogical note: students often conflate 'the wave is dangerous' with 'all waves are dangerous' — stress that the same reflection/diffraction/refraction principles that cause harm are also used in mitigation technology.",
      "explained": "The environmental effects of waves are explained by the same physics principles studied in Lessons 1–5. Coastal erosion at Malindi results from the kinetic energy carried by waves (proportional to amplitude squared) being transferred to the coastline. Tsunami destructiveness results from shoaling — as the wave enters shallow coastal water, its speed decreases (refraction/shoaling), wavelength compresses, but energy is conserved, so amplitude increases dramatically. Diffraction of ocean waves around engineered structures (harbour walls) can inadvertently redirect wave energy into previously sheltered areas — explaining why some mitigation structures create new erosion problems if poorly designed. Seismic wave damage is greatest where S-wave frequency matches the natural resonant frequency of buildings (a wave superposition/resonance effect previewed here). Effective mitigation requires understanding the wave property causing the problem: you cannot design a breakwater without understanding diffraction; you cannot site a sea wall without understanding refraction. Teachers should close with the driving question: 'How does understanding wave behaviour protect Kenyan communities?' — requiring three students to give specific, property-linked answers."
