      "explained": "The final explanatory synthesis connects every lesson back to the driving question. Waves carry energy — not matter — through our environment via particle interactions (Lesson 1). The speed, frequency, and wavelength of a wave are related by v = fλ (Lesson 2). When waves meet boundaries, they reflect (Lesson 3), enabling sonar, ultrasound, and echo-ranging. When wave speed changes at a boundary, waves refract (Lesson 4), bending coastal waves at Diani and causing radio dead zones in the Rift Valley. When waves pass obstacles or gaps comparable to their wavelength, they diffract (Lesson 5), allowing KBC signals to reach remote Kenyan valleys but also focusing wave energy on unprotected shorelines. These properties are harnessed in technology (Lesson 6) and must be understood to design effective environmental protections (Lesson 7). The cumulative student model should now show: a labelled wavefront diagram including arrows for energy direction, particle oscillation arrows (transverse vs longitudinal), boundary interactions (reflection, refraction, diffraction), and at least two annotated Kenyan real-world application examples. Teachers should display two or three exemplary student models and facilitate a class discussion on what makes a model scientifically complete before formally closing the DQB."
